WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:26.460
I'm Robin from Marshmallow Laser Feast,
we're a experiential entity based in London.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:31.710
We're a creative studio exploring
the line between the virtual and the

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:37.380
real we take technologies like virtual
reality and bring them to unexpected

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:42.450
locations. We like to make projects on
the new digital frontier that are

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:47.940
exploring emerging technologies and
embedding them into live interactive

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:59.579
immersive environments. A Colossal Wave
is about human impact. We were

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:04.409
commissioned by the British Council and
Quartier des Spectacles to work with the city

00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:08.130
of Montreal to celebrate 375th birthday.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:12.330
375 years ago was
when Montreal was founded. It was also

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:16.920
the birthday of Isaac Newton and we took
his third law for every action there is

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:21.810
opposed an equal reaction. We started to
talk about the contemporary issues

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.310
facing all of us on this planet and conversations about the great Garbage

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:31.829
Vortex cropped up, climate change,...
received burning forests we see polluted

00:01:31.829 --> 00:01:35.909
seas but that goes over people's heads
and so we wanted to communicate the

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:39.200
ideas in different ways

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:51.250
we wanted to put creation in the hands
of the visitors and what happens is

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.500
there's an indoor experience and an
outdoor experience both connected and

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:01.930
the public are invited to engage in a
mixed reality world this world

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:08.200
celebrates use generated artwork indoor
people sing and create voice fruit

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:25.080
sculptures and these affect the outdoor
experience

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:33.950
and outside what happens is a very
different physical artwork is created a

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.570
physical act of dropping a bowling ball
from a great height and the bowling ball

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:45.110
crashes against a brass disc a gong and
sculpts over a thousand drops the final

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:50.450
gift that was given to the city of
Montreal what's exciting about the new

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:55.880
advances in technology such as virtual
reality is that there's new superpowers

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:01.280
emerging there's new ways to move people
and to engage people and we've only just

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:07.760
really begun to understand the grammar
and the way that we can put these

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:15.770
experiences together in ways that have a
lasting impact on people what we've

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:21.470
tried to do with this piece is provide a
shared VR experience as well in this

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:26.960
case people are connected they can see
each other in VR and they get a sense of

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.720
other activity in the indoor space and
outdoor space as well in different ways

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.640
and we're only just beginning to learn
how best to fine-tune this but one

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:42.350
thing's for sure the sense of excitement
that things like virtual reality

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:46.250
presented to audiences is just
extraordinary

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:51.160
we just can't wait to see what's around
the corner it's moving so rapidly and

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:56.690
hopefully it will develop in a way that
liberates us and allows for more

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:59.590
creative freedom

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.620
you

